      "chunk_id": "text_15",
      "quote": "To assess SARSCoV-2 positivity rates in offspring, we included cohort studies of pregnant and recently pregnant women who sought hospital care for any reason and had a diagnosis of SARS-CoV-2 infection, and where SARSCoV-2 status was ascertained in the fetus or neonate using RT-PCR... or serological tests... We defined cohort studies as those that sampled consecutive women, who were followed-up to ascertain the SARS-CoV-2 status of their offspring within the first 30 days after birth. In addition to the cohort studies, we included case series and case reports to assess the timing of motherto-child transmission and likelihood of infection.",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies the eligible study designs (cohort studies, case series, case reports), the population (pregnant and recently pregnant women with SARS-CoV-2), and follow-up requirements (within the first 30 days after birth)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_14",
      "quote": "No language restrictions were applied.",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies that no language restrictions were applied. While dissemination year and publication status are not explicitly detailed as exclusion criteria, the search strategy covers major databases and preprint servers, implying broad inclusion of report types."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review does not report excluding studies specifically because outcomes were not measured versus not reported; this is a specific methodological distinction not required for all reviews."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "A random effects model was used to synthesise data for rates, with associations reported using odds ratios and 95% confidence intervals. Narrative syntheses were performed when meta-analysis was inappropriate. The World Health Organization classification was used to categorise the timing of mother-to-child transmission (in utero, intrapartum, early postnatal).",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies the synthesis methods (random effects model, narrative synthesis) and the grouping of studies based on the WHO classification for timing of transmission."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "There are no notable restrictions on study eligibility that require a specific rationale beyond standard systematic review inclusion criteria."
    }
  ],
